  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Conditions continue to improve today as the low\nfinally pushes offshore and into the Gulf of Maine.\n\nDamp conditions are slowly beginning to improve as the low \nslowly moves across Cape Cod and offshore into the Gulf of Maine\ntoday. Winds have already begun to transition west-southwest \nacross interior southern New England, but the coastal plain will\nremain misty through sunrise and overcast throughout the day. A\nwarming trend starts today as a warm airmass settles over New \nEngland... surface temperatures today warming to the mid to \nupper 60s, perhaps to just above 70 across the interior where \nthere will be more breaks of sunshine.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Above seasonal temperatures arrive mid-week \nfollowed by unsettled conditions for the weekend.\n\nSurface high pressure and a mid-level ridge begin to build in, \nbringing a warmer and drier airmass to southern New England by \nmid- week. This will enhance the warming trend throughout the \nweek, allowing temperatures to climb into the mid to upper 70s \nby Thursday, possibly near 80 by Friday. The caveat being a low \npressure system moving across Ontario and Quebec Thursday and \nFriday. A trough axis and trailing cold front looks to then \nsweep through New England sometime Friday, bringing the \npotential for rainy and unsettled conditions for the weekend. \nOnce the low moves through Quebec, a rather strong continental \nhigh pressure system will build over Ontario, sinking those +4C \nto +6C 850mb temperatures down into New England and sharply \ndropping surface temperatures through the weekend. The question \nremains when exactly the cold front move through. If it arrives \nearlier Friday, afternoon high temperatures could be mitigated \nby the colder airmass dipping in from Canada.\n\nTowards the second half of the weekend and into early next \nweek, ensemble guidance is suggesting the potential for a \ndeepening low to sweep across the James Bay, bringing a \nsecondary shot of even colder air to New England. 850mb \ntemperatures could drop to near 0C by the start of the next work\nweek, supporting surface highs in the 40s and 50s with \nnighttime lows possibly falling into the 30s depending on how \nfar south the low tracks.",
